UID:20261105T153000Z-2416930@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261105T153000Z
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:Xiao Hai was born in Shangqiu City in Henan Province in China.
  At fifteen he moved to Shenzhen to join the workforce\, moving between ci
 ties and factory jobs while writing poetry in his spare time. He is the au
 thor of the poetry collection Sisyphus on the Wenyu River\, and translatio
 ns of his poetry have appeared in World Literature Today and Chinese Liter
 ature Today.Tony Hao is a New Haven\, Connecticut-based Chinese-to-English
  literary translator and writer. Authors he has translated include Taiwane
 se novelist Tong Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet Xiao Hai 小海\,
  Taiwanese transgender writer Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\, and Chinese short 
 story writer Ban Yu 班宇.  His debut book-length translation\, Adrift in
  the South\, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was published by Granta Magazine Editi
 ons in May\, 2026.  His short-form translations have appeared in Granta\, 
 The Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Review\, The Offing\, Books from Tai
 wan\, Florescence (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, MAYDAY Magazine\, and elsewhe
 re. His work has been recognized by the PEN Translates Grant\, the Granum 
 Foundation Translation Prize\, Art Omi Writers: Translation Lab\, the Verm
 ont Studio Center\, BCLT's Advanced Translation Workshop\, the Bread Loaf 
 Translators' Conference\, and elsewhere.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20261029T153443Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2394847-easc-roundtable-xiao-hai-and
 -tony-hao
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Xiao Hai</strong> was born in Shan
 gqiu City in Henan Province in China. At fifteen he moved to Shenzhen to j
 oin the workforce\, moving between cities and factory jobs while writing p
 oetry in his spare time. He is the author of the poetry collection <i>Sisy
 phus on the Wenyu River</i>\, and translations of his poetry have appeared
  in <i>World Literature Today</i> and <i>Chinese Literature Today</i>.</p>
 <p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>Tony Hao</strong> is a 
 New Haven\, Connecticut-based Chinese-to-English literary translator and w
 riter. Authors he has translated include Taiwanese novelist Tong Wei-Ger 
 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet Xiao Hai 小海\, Taiwanese transgender wr
 iter Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\, and Chinese short story writer Ban Yu 班
 宇.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">  His debut book-length
  translation\, <em>Adrift in the South</em>\, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was p
 ublished by Granta Magazine Editions in May\, 2026.</p><p class="" data-rt
 e-preserve-empty="true">  His short-form translations have appeared in <em
 >Granta</em>\, <em>The Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Review\, The Offi
 ng</em>\, <em>Books from Taiwan\, Florescence (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, M
 AYDAY Magazine</em>\, and elsewhere. His work has been recognized by the P
 EN Translates Grant\, the Granum Foundation Translation Prize\, Art Omi Wr
 iters: Translation Lab\, the Vermont Studio Center\, BCLT's Advanced Trans
 lation Workshop\, the Bread Loaf Translators' Conference\, and elsewhere.<
 /p>
