SUMMARY:IKS Colloquium - Heangjin Park
DTSTART:20261106T170000Z
DTEND:20261106T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153458Z
UID:20261106T170000Z-2402244@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261106T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/177/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,4111,6167/32590_download.rev.1789746129.png
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:IKS Fall 2026 Colloquium SeriesHeangjin Park  Department of As
 ian Languages and Cultures  University of Wisconsin-Madison  "Korean" Kimc
 hi: A National\, Global\, and Diasporic CommodityFriday\, November 612:00-
 1:00pmGISB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave  How has kimchi\, a fermented vegetab
 le dish beloved by Koreans and beyond\, become a national dish that symbol
 izes Korean culture? How has its Koreanness transformed through global pro
 duction and circulation? And what does it mean for the Korean diaspora\, w
 ho navigate political identity and economic opportunity from peripheral po
 sitions? This talk demonstrates how mundane commodities mediate the comple
 x interplay among global capitalism\, nationalism\, and diasporic mobility
  across South Korea and China. Drawing on archival and ethnographic resear
 ch into kimchi's commercial production and circulation\, especially the Ko
 rean Chinese (chosonjok/chaoxianzu) kimchi business\, this talk illuminate
 s the political economic dynamics that reshape the multifaceted implicatio
 ns of "Korea."  Open to the public. A light lunch will be served.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261016T142519Z
LOCATION:355 North Eagleson Avenue
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2394851-iks-colloquium-heangjin-park
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;">  IKS Fall 2
 026 Colloquium Series</h5><h6 style="text-align: center\;"><br /><span cla
 ss="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-feature-calt-o
 ff text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none">Heangjin Park</span></h6>
 <p style="text-align: center\;">  Department of Asian Languages and Cultur
 es<br />  University of Wisconsin-Madison</p><p style="text-align: center\
 ;">  "Korean" Kimchi: A National\, Global\, and Diasporic Commodity</p><p 
 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Friday\, November 6</strong></p><p st
 yle="text-align: center\;"><strong>12:00-1:00pm</strong></p><p style="text
 -align: center\;"><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-c
 lig-off font-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none
 "><strong>GISB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave</strong><br /><br />  How has kim
 chi\, a fermented vegetable dish beloved by Koreans and beyond\, become a 
 national dish that symbolizes Korean culture? How has its Koreanness trans
 formed through global production and circulation? And what does it mean fo
 r the Korean diaspora\, who navigate political identity and economic oppor
 tunity from peripheral positions? This talk demonstrates how mundane commo
 dities mediate the complex interplay among global capitalism\, nationalism
 \, and diasporic mobility across South Korea and China. Drawing on archiva
 l and ethnographic research into kimchi's commercial production and circul
 ation\, especially the Korean Chinese (chosonjok/chaoxianzu) kimchi busine
 ss\, this talk illuminates the political economic dynamics that reshape th
 e multifaceted implications of "Korea."<br /><br />  Open to the public. A
  light lunch will be served.</span></p>
