X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2173974-global-health-research-speaker-series-dr-alka-khaita"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Global Health Research Speaker Series - Dr. Alka Khaitan
DTSTART:20261110T170000Z
DTEND:20261110T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20261016T093852Z
UID:20261110T170000Z-2454052@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261110T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/87/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,1395,592/1038_research_speaker_series_on_white.rev.1565976
 308.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Join the IU Center for Global Health and the Regenstrief Insti
 tute's Center for Health Services Research for this quarter's Global Healt
 h Research Speaker Series session featuring&#160\;Alka Khaitan\, MD (https
 ://medicine.iu.edu/faculty/43672/khaitan-alka)  Professor of Clinical Pedi
 atrics\, Indiana University School of Medicine and Ryan White Center for P
 ediatric Infectious Diseases and Global Health"Beyond Viral Suppression: I
 mmune Dysregulation and HIV Persistence in Perinatal HIV"  Antiretroviral 
 therapy has transformed perinatal HIV into a chronic\, treatable condition
 \, yet viral suppression does not fully restore immune health or eliminate
  persistent HIV reservoirs. This talk will explore the long-term immunolog
 ic consequences of perinatal HIV and how immune dysregulation may intersec
 t with HIV persistence. Research from a Kenyan cohort of children with per
 inatal HIV will provide insight into how the immunologic effects of HIV ac
 quired early in life may persist despite treatment and relate to long-term
  health outcomes and barriers to HIV cure.  Dr. Khaitan is a professor of 
 clinical pediatrics in the Division of Pediatric Infectious Diseases at In
 diana University School of Medicine and the Ryan White Center for Pediatri
 c Infectious Diseases and Global Health. Her research focuses on the long-
 term immunologic consequences of perinatal HIV\, including chronic inflamm
 ation\, immune activation and exhaustion and viral persistence. She leads 
 NIH-funded research in the United States and Kenya examining how the timin
 g and effectiveness of antiretroviral therapy shape immune health and long
 -term outcomes in children with HIV. She is also involved in pediatric HIV
  cure research through the IMPAACT Network and serves on the U.S. Departme
 nt of Health and Human Services Pediatric Antiretroviral Guidelines Panel.
   This session is being held both virtually (via Teams webinar) and in-per
 son. If you plan to attend the session in-person at the Regenstrief Instit
 ute you must register by Thursday\, November 5th at 10 am Eastern Time and
  indicate this choice when you register. &#160\;  Additionally\, please ta
 ke note of the following in-person details:In-Person Location: Regenstrief
  Institute (Room 209/210)\, 1101 W. 10th Street\, Indianapolis (corner of 
 10th Street and Wilson Street)  Parking: Public (for fee) parking is avail
 able in the Wilson Street Garage which is adjacent to the building.  Build
 ing Security: Regenstrief maintains a secure building entrance. Event supp
 ort from the Center for Global Health and the Center for Health Services R
 esearch will be onsite to assist with entry and other event logistics.
GEO:39.779628;-86.180457
LAST-MODIFIED:20261016T133852Z
LOCATION:Teams Webinar &amp\; The Regenstrief Institute
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2173974-global-health-research-speak
 er-series-dr-alka-khaita
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join the IU Center for Global Health and
  the Regenstrief Institute's Center for Health Services Research for this 
 quarter's Global Health Research Speaker Series session featuring&#160\;</
 p><p><a href="https://medicine.iu.edu/faculty/43672/khaitan-alka">Alka Kha
 itan\, MD</a></p><p>  Professor of Clinical Pediatrics\, Indiana Universit
 y School of Medicine and Ryan White Center for Pediatric Infectious Diseas
 es and Global Health</p><p><strong>"Beyond Viral Suppression: Immune Dysre
 gulation and HIV Persistence in Perinatal HIV"</strong></p><p>  Antiretrov
 iral therapy has transformed perinatal HIV into a chronic\, treatable cond
 ition\, yet viral suppression does not fully restore immune health or elim
 inate persistent HIV reservoirs. This talk will explore the long-term immu
 nologic consequences of perinatal HIV and how immune dysregulation may int
 ersect with HIV persistence. Research from a Kenyan cohort of children wit
 h perinatal HIV will provide insight into how the immunologic effects of H
 IV acquired early in life may persist despite treatment and relate to long
 -term health outcomes and barriers to HIV cure.</p><p>  Dr. Khaitan is a p
 rofessor of clinical pediatrics in the Division of Pediatric Infectious Di
 seases at Indiana University School of Medicine and the Ryan White Center 
 for Pediatric Infectious Diseases and Global Health. Her research focuses 
 on the long-term immunologic consequences of perinatal HIV\, including chr
 onic inflammation\, immune activation and exhaustion and viral persistence
 . She leads NIH-funded research in the United States and Kenya examining h
 ow the timing and effectiveness of antiretroviral therapy shape immune hea
 lth and long-term outcomes in children with HIV. She is also involved in p
 ediatric HIV cure research through the IMPAACT Network and serves on the U
 .S. Department of Health and Human Services Pediatric Antiretroviral Guide
 lines Panel.</p><p>  This session is being held both virtually (via Teams 
 webinar) and in-person. If you plan to attend the session in-person at the
  Regenstrief Institute you must register by Thursday\, November 5th at 10 
 am Eastern Time and indicate this choice when you register. &#160\;</p><p>
   Additionally\, please take note of the following in-person details:</p><
 ul><li>In-Person Location: Regenstrief Institute (Room 209/210)\, 1101 W. 
 10th Street\, Indianapolis (corner of 10th Street and Wilson Street)  </li
 ><li>Parking: Public (for fee) parking is available in the Wilson Street G
 arage which is adjacent to the building.  </li><li>Building Security: Rege
 nstrief maintains a secure building entrance. Event support from the Cente
 r for Global Health and the Center for Health Services Research will be on
 site to assist with entry and other event logistics.  </li></ul>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Vanessa Kleinschmidt<br />\n  vkleins@iu.e
 du\n</p>
