BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 64": "https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2464840-ijgls-symposium"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_64
SUMMARY:IJGLS Symposium
DTSTART:20261120T144500Z
DTEND:20261120T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20261027T100702Z
UID:20261120T144500Z-2464840@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261120T144500Z
DESCRIPTION:Climate change\, pollution\, and biodiversity loss are intensi
 fying worldwide\, and their heaviest burdens fall on the world's most vuln
 erable populations and indigenous communities. An estimated 1.5 billion pe
 ople lack basic access to redress for environmental harms through politica
 l\, administrative\, or legal channels. Many of these communities also lac
 k skilled advocates\, and the lawyers and nonlawyers who do take up their 
 causes face growing harassment and physical attacks. The Indiana Journal o
 f Global Legal Studies has brought together scholars from across the Ameri
 cas to examine where marginalized communities\, their advocates\, and the 
 governance systems that too often leave them behind intersect.  &#160\;Pan
 el 1: Environmental Protection Across Borders: Who Shapes the Rules\, and 
 Who Gets Heard?Panel 2: Effective Environmental Representation: Bridging t
 he Gap Between Rights and ResultsPanel 3: Environmental Governance Framewo
 rks: What Do Comprehensive Solutions Look Like?Panel 4: In Practice: Apply
 ing Environmental Rights and Supporting Environmental Defenders  Papers pr
 esented at the symposium will be published in Volume 34 Issue 2 of the Jou
 rnal.&#160\;
GEO:39.165074;-86.526424
LAST-MODIFIED:20261029T150243Z
LOCATION:LOWELL E. BAIER HALL/IU MAURER SCHOOL OF LAW
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2464840-ijgls-symposium
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Climate change\, pollution\, and biodive
 rsity loss are intensifying worldwide\, and their heaviest burdens fall on
  the world's most vulnerable populations and indigenous communities. An es
 timated 1.5 billion people lack basic access to redress for environmental 
 harms through political\, administrative\, or legal channels. Many of thes
 e communities also lack skilled advocates\, and the lawyers and nonlawyers
  who do take up their causes face growing harassment and physical attacks.
  The <em>Indiana Journal of Global Legal Studies</em> has brought together
  scholars from across the Americas to examine where marginalized communiti
 es\, their advocates\, and the governance systems that too often leave the
 m behind intersect.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>Panel 1:</strong> Enviro
 nmental Protection Across Borders: Who Shapes the Rules\, and Who Gets Hea
 rd?</p><p><strong>Panel 2:</strong> Effective Environmental Representation
 : Bridging the Gap Between Rights and Results</p><p><strong>Panel 3:</stro
 ng> Environmental Governance Frameworks: What Do Comprehensive Solutions L
 ook Like?</p><p><strong>Panel 4:</strong> In Practice: Applying Environmen
 tal Rights and Supporting Environmental Defenders</p><p>  Papers presented
  at the symposium will be published in Volume 34 Issue 2 of the Journal.&#
 160\;</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Sophia (Sophie) Carmosino\, Editor-In-Chie
 f<br />\n  <a href="mailto:scarmos@iu.edu">scarmos@iu.edu</a>\n</p>
