X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_11
SUMMARY:Kristýna Milde | Forest as Home: Landscape\, Memory\, Connection 
 | Lecture
DTSTART:20261120T220000Z
DTEND:20261120T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20260922T161359Z
UID:20261120T220000Z-2426888@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261120T220000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,768,568/32669_Kristyna-and-Marek-Milde-Arbomobile-photo-by
 -Hudacinova-WEB.rev.1790107452.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:In this talk\, Kristýna Milde shares their 15-year investigat
 ion into how trees\, forests\, newspapers\, architecture\, urban infrastru
 cture\, and living materials can become sites for questioning the boundari
 es between nature and culture and the search for connection.&#160\;  The C
 zech-American interdisciplinary artist duo based in Brooklyn\, New York wo
 rks across sculpture\, site-specific installation\, photography\, and anim
 ation\, combining living plants\, transformed materials\, and everyday obj
 ects to create evolving environments that reveal the interconnectedness of
  forests\, cities\, and the cultural spaces we inhabit.  Recipients of the
  2026 NYSCA/NYFA Artist Fellowship in Sculpture\, their work has been feat
 ured in The New York Times\, The Brooklyn Rail\, Flash Art\, Hyperal
 lergic\, Forbes\, and Czech television and radio\, among others. The C
 ollege of Arts and Sciences and the Eskenazi School of Art\, Architecture 
 + Design gratefully acknowledge Dr. Meredith McKinney (B.A. '65) and Elsa 
 Luise Barthel McKinney (B.A. '65) for their love of the arts and their gen
 erous support in endowing the McKinney Visiting Artist Series at Indiana U
 niversity Bloomington.
GEO:39.169084;-86.518997
LAST-MODIFIED:20261012T184732Z
LOCATION:Fine Arts Building\, 1201 East 7th Street\, Bloomington\, IN 4740
 5
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2426888-kristyna-milde-forest-as-
 home-landscape-memory
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1">  In this talk\, Kristýna Mild
 e shares their 15-year investigation into how trees\, forests\, newspapers
 \, architecture\, urban infrastructure\, and living materials can become s
 ites for questioning the boundaries between nature and culture and the sea
 rch for connection.&#160\;</p><p class="p1">  The Czech-American interdisc
 iplinary artist duo based in Brooklyn\, New York works across sculpture\, 
 site-specific installation\, photography\, and animation\, combining livin
 g plants\, transformed materials\, and everyday objects to create evolving
  environments that reveal the interconnectedness of forests\, cities\, and
  the cultural spaces we inhabit.</p><p class="p1">  Recipients of the 2026
  NYSCA/NYFA Artist Fellowship in Sculpture\, their work has been featured 
 in The New York Times\, The Brooklyn Rail\, Flash Art\, Hyperallergi
 c\, Forbes\, and Czech television and radio\, among others. </p><p cla
 ss="p1"><em>The College of Arts and Sciences and the Eskenazi School of Ar
 t\, Architecture + Design gratefully acknowledge Dr. Meredith McKinney (B.
 A. '65) and Elsa Luise Barthel McKinney (B.A. '65) for their love of the a
 rts and their generous support in endowing the McKinney Visiting Artist Se
 ries at Indiana University Bloomington.</em></p>
