SUMMARY:Frances Lightbound: The Land Dissolves in Water | Exhibition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261122
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261123
DTSTAMP:20260909T104718Z
UID:20261122T050000Z-2412144@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20261122
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,768,1051/32427_Frances-Lightbound-Underlay-Topographies-II
 I-2026-graphite-rubbing-on-paper-WEB.rev.1788964548.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Lectures, talks, seminars, and confe
 rences
DESCRIPTION:Lecture: October 27\, 6:30–8pmExhibition: October 27–Decem
 ber 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in session  Lig
 htbound studies the production of space in urban environments\, weaving na
 rratives across photography\, print\, spatial interventions\, and sculptur
 e\, with a focus on materials and sites in transition.  Architecture becom
 es an entry-point to questions of land use\, ownership and access\, bodily
  experience and perception\, and timeframes ranging from the human to the 
 geological. Lightbound invites viewers to consider and participate in the 
 continual (re)construction of meaning in urban environments.  Frances Ligh
 tbound holds a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotland\, and an M.F.A. 
 from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent solo exhibitions i
 nclude Louise Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, TX)\, RULE Gallery (M
 arfa\, TX)\, and the John David Mooney Foundation (Chicago\, IL)\; her wor
 k has been exhibited internationally at venues in London\, Glasgow and Ber
 lin. Lightbound is a Lecturer at School of the Art Institute of Chicago in
  the departments of Print media and Photography.The J. Irwin Miller Archit
 ecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series brings prominent working arti
 sts and architects to Columbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium and 
 exhibitions in the gallery\, both located within The Republic Building\, 3
 33 Second Street in Columbus. The gallery is open Monday through Friday fr
 om 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are free and open to the public.
GEO:39.199929;-85.921713
LAST-MODIFIED:20260910T162141Z
LOCATION:The Republic Building
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2412144-frances-lightbound-the-la
 nd-dissolves-in-water-exhib
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1"><strong>Lecture:</strong> Octob
 er 27\, 6:30–8pm</p><p class="p1"><strong>Exhibition:</strong> October 2
 7–December 6\, 2026\, Mon.–Thur. 10am–4pm when IU classes are in ses
 sion</p><p class="p1">  Lightbound studies the production of space in urba
 n environments\, weaving narratives across photography\, print\, spatial i
 nterventions\, and sculpture\, with a focus on materials and sites in tran
 sition.</p><p class="p1">  Architecture becomes an entry-point to question
 s of land use\, ownership and access\, bodily experience and perception\, 
 and timeframes ranging from the human to the geological. Lightbound invite
 s viewers to consider and participate in the continual (re)construction of
  meaning in urban environments.</p><p class="p1">  Frances Lightbound hold
 s a B.A. from Glasgow School of Art\, Scotland\, and an M.F.A. from the Sc
 hool of the Art Institute of Chicago. Recent solo exhibitions include Loui
 se Hopkins Center for the Arts (Lubbock\, TX)\, RULE Gallery (Marfa\, TX)\
 , and the John David Mooney Foundation (Chicago\, IL)\; her work has been 
 exhibited internationally at venues in London\, Glasgow and Berlin. Lightb
 ound is a Lecturer at School of the Art Institute of Chicago in the depart
 ments of Print media and Photography.</p><p class="p1"><em>The J. Irwin Mi
 ller Architecture Program Lecture and Exhibition Series brings prominent w
 orking artists and architects to Columbus. Lectures take place in the audi
 torium and exhibitions in the gallery\, both located within The Republic B
 uilding\, 333 Second Street in Columbus. The gallery is open Monday throug
 h Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All events are free and open to the public.
 </em></p>
