DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261205
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261206
DTSTAMP:20260914T120832Z
UID:20261031T040000Z-2417007@events.iu.edu__20261205
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,551,551/31917_franklin_button_2.rev.1786550505.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you to The Declaration of I
 ndependence: The Signers\, an exhibition of letters and documents bearing 
 the autographs of the fifty-six men who signed the August 2\, 1776 handwri
 tten copy of the Declaration. Though the length and content of these lette
 rs and documents vary widely\, from brief notes or orders to long and deta
 iled personal letters\, they all offer insight into the daily lives\, acti
 vities\, and concerns of the signers. One aspect of their lives that these
  documents don't reveal\, however\, is the ownership of slaves by a great 
 many of the signers\, which is discussed in the "Race\, Slavery\, and Nati
 ve Americans" section of the exhibition in the Lilly Library's North Galle
 ry\, The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment. (https://l
 ibraries.indiana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence)  Documents si
 gned by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are displayed in the Lilly Lib
 rary's South Gallery. Documents signed by the two other signers—Philip L
 ivingston of New York and James Smith of Pennsylvania—are on display in 
 the North Gallery in The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Mo
 ment.
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
LAST-MODIFIED:20260914T160832Z
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2379077-the-declaration-of-independe
 nce-the-signers
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you t
 o <em>The Declaration of Independence: The Signers</em>\, an exhibition of
  letters and documents bearing the autographs of the fifty-six men who sig
 ned the August 2\, 1776 handwritten copy of the Declaration. Though the le
 ngth and content of these letters and documents vary widely\, from brief n
 otes or orders to long and detailed personal letters\, they all offer insi
 ght into the daily lives\, activities\, and concerns of the signers. One a
 spect of their lives that these documents don't reveal\, however\, is the 
 ownership of slaves by a great many of the signers\, which is discussed in
  the "Race\, Slavery\, and Native Americans" section of the exhibition in 
 the Lilly Library's North Gallery\, <em><a href="https://libraries.indiana
 .edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence" target="_blank" rel="noopener
 ">The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment.</a></em></p><
 p>  Documents signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are displayed 
 in the Lilly Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by the two other si
 gners—Philip Livingston of New York and James Smith of Pennsylvania—ar
 e on display in the North Gallery in <a href="https://libraries.indiana.ed
 u/lilly-library/declaration-independence" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><
 em>The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment</em></a>.</p>
